Make rlua 0.15.4-alpha.0 (78021185) compile on a modern Rust toolchain.

WHAT THIS CHANGES, AND WHY IT DOES NOT HIDE THE BUG
===================================================
The `rlua_panic!` macro expands to `panic!(...);` -- with a trailing semicolon --
and is then invoked in expression position (e.g. `_ => rlua_panic!("...")` in
src/lua.rs:728). Rust deprecated that in 2020 (rust-lang issue #79813) and it is
now a hard error, so the crate does not build at all: 6 errors, all from this one
macro body.

The patch removes the trailing semicolon from the two `panic!` arms, making the
macro a valid expression. It touches only panic formatting in an internal
assertion macro -- no lifetime, ownership, borrow, or Lua-state logic -- and none
of the affected call sites are on the path this row exercises.

The defect under test is the unconstrained `'callback` lifetime in
`Lua::create_function`'s signature (src/lua.rs), which this patch does not touch.

